<Docs
  title="Install — PhoenixSEO Page Builder docs"
  description="Requirements, installation and activation of the PhoenixSEO Page Builder, and a tour of the component screen it adds to wp-admin."
  path="/docs/install/"
  doc="install"
  lede="Chapter 02"
>
  <h1>Install</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    One plugin, one dependency, one new screen. Fifteen minutes if PHP and the file system behave, and they
    usually do.
  </p>

  <h2>What it needs</h2>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Requirement</th><th>Why</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>WordPress 6.4 or newer</td>
        <td>The plugin registers its block category through <code>block_categories_all</code>, which needs 5.8; 6.4 is where the tested baseline sits.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>PHP 7.4 or newer</td>
        <td>Typed properties and arrow functions are used throughout.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>ACF or Secure Custom Fields, active</td>
        <td>They provide the field types and the admin UI for filling them in. The plugin checks for <code>acf_get_field_group()</code>; SCF answers to the same function because it stays drop-in compatible.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>A writable <code>wp-content/sdc-components/</code></td>
        <td>Only if you want to create, duplicate or delete components in wp-admin. Shipped components work without it.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Install the plugin</h2>

  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      <div>
        <strong>Get the ZIP.</strong> Either the packaged plugin, or — if you work from the repository —
        the <code>plugin/</code> directory, zipped so that the archive contains a single folder.
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div>
        <strong>Upload it.</strong> <em>Plugins → Add New → Upload Plugin → Choose file → Install Now</em>.
        On a host without upload rights, unpack it into <code>wp-content/plugins/</code> instead; the result
        is the same directory.
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div>
        <strong>Activate.</strong> The plugin needs no settings screen and writes nothing on activation.
        Its components are read from the file system on every request that needs them.
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div>
        <strong>Check the menu.</strong> A new entry <em>Page Builder</em> sits in the sidebar. It is the
        only screen the plugin adds.
      </div>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>The component screen</h2>

  <p>
    <em>Page Builder</em> opens a page titled <em>PhoenixSEO Components</em>. It has three sections, one
    below the other: the catalog, a form for a new component, and an import form. No tabs — the page is
    long, and every section is reachable by scrolling or by the browser's own find.
  </p>

  <figure class="shot">
    <img
      src="/docs/screen-components.webp"
      width="1400"
      height="861"
      alt="The PhoenixSEO Components screen in wp-admin: a table of components with columns for fields, template, storage and usage, each row offering Export and Duplicate."
    />
    <figcaption>
      The catalog. <code>Plugin</code> in the Storage column means the component ships with the plugin and
      is read-only here; <code>wp-content</code> means it is a site component and can be edited.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>One row per component, six columns:</p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Column</th><th>Shows</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Component</td>
        <td>The name from the definition, its type as a slug, and the description — the same text the inserter shows.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Fields</td>
        <td>How many fields the component defines. Nested fields count once at the top level.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Template</td>
        <td>The <code>render.php</code> being used, or a dash when the component renders generically.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Storage</td>
        <td>Which directory the definition came from, and whether it can be edited here.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Usage</td>
        <td>How many pages and instances use it. <em>not placed</em> means no page has it yet.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Actions</td>
        <td><em>Edit</em> for site components, <em>Export</em> and <em>Duplicate</em> for all of them, <em>Delete</em> for site components.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>When something is locked</h2>

  <p>
    Two conditions switch parts of the screen off, and both say so at the top of the page rather than
    failing on submit:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The target folder is not writable.</strong> The catalog is still shown, but creating,
      duplicating, importing and deleting are locked. Fix the permissions on
      <code>wp-content/sdc-components/</code> — the plugin prints the path it tried.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A page has lost a component.</strong> If a component disappears while pages still reference
      it, those pages render nothing where it used to be. The plugin counts them and points at
      <code>wp sdc-pb repair</code>, which reports and, with <code>--apply</code>, re-attaches what is
      missing. It is a WP-CLI command on purpose: writing to other people's pages from a button is a
      decision for the site owner.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Next</h2>

  <p>
    Nothing is on a page yet. <a href="/docs/use/">Chapter 03</a> puts a component there.
  </p>
